## Classify

Structured LLM classification: give it `criteria` (the domain rules — allowed labels, scoring scale, which signals matter) and `facts` (a JSON object describing one item), and it returns a parsed `{ label, score, rationale }` instead of raw model text.

```ts
import ClassifyCommand from '../classify';
let result = await new ClassifyCommand(commandContext).execute({
  criteria: LEAD_SCORING_CRITERIA,
  facts: JSON.stringify({ source, status, campaign }),
});
lead.score = result.score;
```

**The block does not know what a lead is.** The classifier contract — apply the caller's criteria to the caller's facts, answer in one JSON shape — is all it carries. Revenue OS supplies lead-scoring criteria (status progression, source/campaign agreement, campaign health, firmographics) and writes the returned score back onto the Lead; a different consumer could triage support tickets or grade invoice risk with the same command.

The command parses defensively: it extracts the outermost JSON object from the model output, throws with the raw text when parsing fails, and returns only typed fields — so the consumer never string-matches model prose. `llmModel` is overridable and defaults to the platform's coding model.

The facts payload is built by the consumer from what it has actually loaded — the command never fetches links itself, so the caller decides which related facts (here, the lead's campaign) are in evidence.

The linked example is the command's natural target: a qualified webinar lead attributed to a webinar campaign, where source and campaign agree and the score should come out hot.

**Non-goals:** deciding the criteria (the consumer's domain knowledge), writing the result anywhere (the consumer patches its own card), batch classification, and confidence calibration across runs — one item in, one judgment out.
